{'chapter_id': 6, 'bundle_token': 'b6-a577c856', 'content': 'The Ring of Nestor
has been discussed in modern scholarship primarily as part of a wider corpus of
Minoan-style gold signet rings that includes securely excavated examples from 
sites such as Pylos, Mycenae, and other centres in western 
Messenia.[2][4][5][9] In their publications on the Griffin Warrior’s grave near
the Palace of Nestor at Pylos, Jack L. Davis and Shari Stocker emphasize four 
gold signet rings decorated with Minoan ritual scenes and argue that these, 
together with other Minoan-style grave goods, provide evidence for intensive 
cultural interaction between Cretan and mainland elites in the fifteenth 
century BCE.[2][3][4][9] The Ring of Nestor is often cited in this context as 
another large gold signet with elaborate ritual imagery that can be compared 
stylistically to the Pylos rings and to other Minoan-style seals and rings used
to explore ritual, ideology, and artistic technique in the Late Bronze Age 
Aegean.[1][2][4][6][9]\n\nThe excavation of the Griffin Warrior’s tomb and the 
publication of the gold rings and the Pylos Combat Agate have renewed interest 
in miniature Aegean art and have encouraged re-examination of older finds, 
including the Ring of Nestor, whose provenance and authenticity are less 
securely documented.[2][4][5][9] Dimitrios S. Dendrinos’s analysis of the 
5‑priestess ring from the Griffin Warrior’s tomb, focusing on its 
near-elliptical bezel and proposed astronomical and fertility-related 
iconography, illustrates how highly detailed geometric and iconographic studies
of one ring can influence approaches to other examples.[10] Project reports and
popular accounts of the Griffin Warrior’s grave point out that the four signet 
rings and other Minoan-style objects functioned as symbols of political power 
and as material expressions of connections between mainland and Cretan elites, 
an interpretation also highlighted by Cynthia W. Shelmerdine.[3][4][5][10] 
Against this background, the Ring of Nestor is used in discussions of how 
specific rings, whether securely excavated or not, contribute to 
reconstructions of elite display, ritual practice, and cross-cultural 
interaction in the Aegean Bronze Age.\n\nOutside specialist literature, the 
Ring of Nestor appears in museum captions and commercial image databases, which
describe it as a Late Minoan gold signet ring from Kakovatos or the wider 
region of Pylos, showing four cult scenes and weighing around 31.8 grams.[6] 
These descriptions place the ring alongside other high-status objects from the 
second millennium BCE Aegean that are used in exhibitions, educational 
materials, and general overviews to illustrate Minoan-style goldwork and the 
use of engraved signet rings in administrative and ritual contexts.[4][6][9] 
The combination of scholarly debate over authenticity and frequent visual 
reproduction in museum and media settings has made the Ring of Nestor a 
recurring reference point in discussions of both the possibilities and the 
limits of using unprovenanced objects to study prehistoric Aegean religion and 
art.[1][6]\n', 'evidence_refs': ['e9', 'e58', 'c1', 'c2', 'c3', 'c4', 'c7', 
